well we're always hopeful and uh as you  know the negotiations are continuing  uh we've got our teams still out there  in brussels  and uh you know if there's if there's uh  a big offer a big change in what they're  saying then i must say  that i've yet to see it unfortunately at  the moment as as you know there are two  key things where we  we just can't seem to uh to make  progress and that's the  it's kind of ratchet clause they've got  in to keep the uk  uh locked in uh to whatever they want to  to do in terms of  legislation which obviously doesn't work  and then there's the the whole issue of  fish  where you know we've got to be able to  take back control of our of our waters  so  there's a way to go uh we're hopeful  that progress  can be made but i've got to tell you  that from where i stand now  uh here in blithe uh it's looking it's  looking you know  very very likely that we'll have to go  for  a solution that i think would be you  know  wonderful for the uk we'd be able to do  exactly what we want from  uh from january the first obviously it  would be uh  different from what we'd set out to  achieve but i've no doubt that this  country  can get ready and as i say come out on  world trade terms  we speak to our political correspondent  tom brainer who's in westminster for us  about this  boris johnson trying to put a gloss on  the no deal  reality that's staring us in the face if  they aren't able to make this deal by  sunday a little bit more optimistic than  what we heard  uh this morning from a government  minister who said we'll be able to  survive not exactly  a ringing endorsement yeah i think there  was sort of three  notable points from that statement from  the prime minister the first the  implication  uh that as far as he is concerned it is  for the eu  to move and to offer uh something rather  than the uk government when he said if  there is a big offer or a big change  in what they are saying then i need i'm  yet to see it i think that is something  where  uh you know may that may raise some  eyebrows in brussels who feel that  it's the uk that needs to uh make some  move if there is to be some form of  agreement i think the other thing that  was interesting  is that he referred to this the aspect  of disagreement on the  level playing field which is the degree  to which uh the uk and the eu would  agree  to maintain uh standards of regulations  he said  describe the ratchet clause as being  something that keeps the uk locked into  whatever they want to do in terms of  legislation  which obviously doesn't work well i  think we'll hear more from michelle in a  moment in brussels about this but  earlier  deny and the eu representative there was  saying that  ultimately the uk would be able to  choose not to follow uh  in the steps that the eu takes it would  simply be the case that that decision  would  incur some consequence in terms of the  market but of course the uk says that  that's a false choice because if  the eu were able to wield the threat of  tariffs  in the event that the uk didn't follow  its regulatory  changes then that is akin uh to being  forced to follow  so i think there what you're seeing is  the contours of where that dispute is  in other words how do people does either  side determine  what is a regulation they have to follow  and should it be  a unilateral decision of the eu to say  that the uk must follow or face  reduced market access or tariffs or  should it be something that goes via  some form of arbitration i think these  are the areas where people  are going to be discussing potential  ways forward over the next few days and  of course sunday has been identified  both by the uk government and indeed the  eu  as a point at which they need to take an  assessment as to whether or not there is  any  reason or point to continuing talks but  uh fundamentally it's worth noting that  ultimately what they're saying is  uh whether the assessment on sunday will  be about whether the conditions  are there to continue talks they're not  saying there has to be a deal itself  agreed uh or by sunday which is uh what  some people have  it seems interpreted from what we've  heard in the last few days one final  point i think just worth pointing out  is at the end there boris johnson said  uh that if a trade deal  isn't agreed then the uk uh will be able  to come out on world  trade terms now of course in the last  few days in fact the last few weeks he's  been referring to it as  australia-style arrangements uh been  accused  uh by his critics of trying to put a  positive gloss on what would be  leaving without a trade deal now  following some comments by malcolm  turnbull the former australian prime  minister last night who said that the  australian terms  of trade with the eu weren't necessarily  all that good  i think it's interesting that boris  johnson appears to have dropped that  reference point  is now explicitly saying world trade  organization terms
 

